--- !u!114 &1952198555
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1952198554}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f80d75fe23ffd5645aabe8e618b01b9b, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  passageViewPanner: {fileID: 1952198557}
  confidencePanner: {fileID: 710997370}
  confidenceManager: {fileID: 710997368}
  defaultCameraSize: 535
  allPassages:
  - passageHeading: 'Hurricanes: Powerful Storms of the Ocean'
    passageBody: "A hurricane is a large, powerful storm that forms over warm ocean
      water. Warm, moist air rises into the sky. Cooler air moves in to take its
      place. This creates an area of low air pressure. The air begins to spin because
      of Earth\u2019s rotation. Warm water gives the storm energy to grow stronger.
      This energy helps create strong winds, heavy rain, and thunderstorms.\n\r\nScientists
      measure hurricanes using the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale. This scale
      has five categories based on wind speed. A Category 1 hurricane has winds of
      74 to 95 miles per hour. It can damage trees, power lines, and gutters. A Category
      2 hurricane has winds of 96 to 110 miles per hour. It can cause serious damage
      to houses and roads. Category 3 hurricanes are called major hurricanes. Their
      winds reach 111 to 129 miles per hour. They may cause power and water outages.\n\r\nCategory
      4 hurricanes have winds of 130 to 156 miles per hour. They can destroy parts
      of houses and neighborhoods. Category 5 hurricanes have winds of 157 miles
      per hour or more. They are the strongest hurricanes. They can cause extreme
      damage. After a Category 5 hurricane, some areas may be unsafe for weeks or
      months.\n\r\nHurricanes are most common in tropical and subtropical regions.
      These areas have warm ocean water that gives storms energy. In the Atlantic
      Ocean, hurricanes often form in the Caribbean and the Gulf of America. They
      also form near the eastern coast of the United States. The same types of storms
      happen in the Pacific and Indian Ocean. They also happen near Australia. People
      in these places may call them typhoons or tropical cyclones. \n\r\nScientists
      use satellites, airplanes, and weather tools to track hurricanes. They use
      this information to predict where storms may go. Learning about hurricanes
      helps people prepare for dangerous weather. People can make emergency plans
      and protect their homes. They can stay safe when storms approach.\r\n"
    questions:
    - questionText: What causes a hurricane to form? (1 point)
      answers:
      - Cold air and sinking dry air
      - Warm ocean water and rising moist air
      - Strong mountain winds and cool dry air
      - Heavy snowfall and freezing cold ocean water
    - questionText: 'What does the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale measure? (1
        point) '
      answers:
      - The amount of rain a hurricane brings
      - The total area a hurricane spreads across
      - The wind speed and strength of a hurricane
      - The number of days a hurricane lasts
  - passageHeading: 'Weather Tools: Mini Passages'
    passageBody: "Meteorologists are scientists who study and predict the weather.
      They use special tools to collect weather data.\r\n\r\nThermometer:\r\n\r\nA
      thermometer measures temperature, or how hot or cold something is. Everything
      is made of tiny particles that are always moving. When something gets hotter,
      its particles move faster. This motion is called kinetic energy. Many thermometers
      have a thin tube with red liquid inside. When the liquid gets warmer, its particles
      move faster and spread apart. The liquid takes up more space, so it rises in
      the tube. Temperature is measured in degrees Fahrenheit (\xB0F) or Celsius
      (\xB0C). Meteorologists use temperature along with other weather data. For
      example, they look at where air masses are forming. This information can help
      them predict storms such as tornadoes and hurricanes. \r\n\r\nAnemometer:\r\n\r\nAn
      anemometer measures wind speed, or how fast the wind is blowing. Most anemometers
      have three to four cups attached to a rod. When the wind blows, it pushes the
      cups and makes them spin.  The stronger the wind, the faster the cups spin.
      Wind speed can be calculated by counting how many times the anemometer spins
      in a certain amount of time. A sudden change in wind speed may signal thunderstorms,
      hurricanes, or tornadoes. \r\n\r\nRain gauge:\r\n\r\nA rain gauge measures
      precipitation level, or how much rain has fallen in an area. A rain gauge is
      a tall, thin container placed outside to collect rain. It should be placed
      in a clear area away from trees and buildings. Trees or buildings can block
      some of the rain and make the measurement less accurate. After a certain amount
      of time, the water in the container is measured. This tells us how much rain
      has fallen. It can also help meteorologists warn people about floods. \r\n\r\nHygrometer:
      \r\n\r\nA hygrometer measures the amount of moisture in the air. This amount
      of moisture in the air is called humidity. Some hygrometers have a material
      inside, such as paper or hair, that takes in moisture. When the air is humid,
      the material takes in more water. When the air is dry, it takes in less. A
      dial on the hygrometer shows the humidity level. High humidity can be a sign
      that rain is coming. Measuring humidity can also help meteorologists predict
      dangerous weather.\r\n\r\nWind vane:\r\n\r\nA wind vane measures the direction
      the wind is blowing. We describe wind direction using north, south, east, and
      west. These are called cardinal directions. A wind vane is shaped like an arrow
      and turns freely on a pole. The arrow points toward the direction the wind
      is coming from. We name the wind based on where it comes from. For example,
      a north wind comes from the north.\r\n\r\nWind direction helps meteorologists
      track fronts. A front is the place where a warm air mass and a cold air mass
      meet. Knowing where a front is moving can help predict which places a storm
      may reach.\r\n\r\nBarometer:\r\n\r\nA barometer measures air pressure. Air
      pressure is the force of air pressing down on Earth\u2019s surface. Changes
      in air pressure can help us predict the weather. Low air pressure can be a
      sign of wind, rain, or storms. If the pressure drops quickly, a stronger storm
      may be coming. When air pressure rises, the weather is often clearer and calmer.\r"
    questions:
    - questionText: What does an anemometer measure? (1 point)
      answers:
      - Wind direction
      - Air pressure
      - Wind speed
      - Humidity
